patch: don't crash on a hunk starting with "\ No newline".
A "\ No newline at end of file" line marks the hunk line before it. The
check for that line tested TT.current_hunk->prev, but the hunk is a
circular list, so prev is never NULL when the hunk has a line, and when
it has none TT.current_hunk itself is NULL. A hunk whose first body
line was a "\" line dereferenced the NULL pointer: patch crashed and
left its temporary copy of the file behind.

Test TT.current_hunk instead, so that the line is ignored when there
is no previous line to mark, as the comment intends.

---
 editors/patch.c |    2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/editors/patch.c
+++ b/editors/patch.c
@@ -439,7 +439,7 @@
 			case '\\':
 				// '\ No newline at end of file' detected, mark
 				// previous line, if it exists.
-				if (TT.current_hunk->prev)
+				if (TT.current_hunk)
 					TT.current_hunk->prev->no_newline = TRUE;
 				free(patchline);
 				continue;
